#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703690 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- x y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(e^{x - y^{2}} + 1 \\right)},\n$$\n\nevaluate both partial derivatives at $(1,2)$.\n\n$f_x(1,2)=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$f_y(1,2)=$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x - y^{2}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x y^{2}\\right)&=- y^{2}\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- y^{2} + \\frac{e^{x - y^{2}}}{e^{x - y^{2}} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(1\\right) - \\left(2\\right)^{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(1,2)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(-4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{\\left(e^{-3} + 1\\right) e^{3}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(-4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{- 4 e^{3} - 3}{1 + e^{3}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTreat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x - y^{2}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=- 2 y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x y^{2}\\right)&=- 2 x y\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 2 y\\right)+\\left(- 2 x y\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 x y - \\frac{2 y e^{x - y^{2}}}{e^{x - y^{2}} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(1\\right) - \\left(2\\right)^{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(1,2)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-4\\right)+\\left(-4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{\\left(e^{-3} + 1\\right) e^{3}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-4\\right)+\\left(-4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{4 \\left(- e^{3} - 2\\right)}{1 + e^{3}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703699 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^{2} y + \\ln{\\left(e^{x^{2} + y} + 1 \\right)},\n$$\n\nevaluate both partial derivatives at $(1,2)$.\n\n$f_x(1,2)=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$f_y(1,2)=$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x^{2} + y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(x^{2} y\\right)&=2 x y\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 x\\right)+\\left(2 x y\\right)\\\\\n&=2 x y + \\frac{2 x e^{x^{2} + y}}{e^{x^{2} + y} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(1\\right)^{2} + \\left(2\\right)=3.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(1,2)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e^{3}}{1 + e^{3}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 \\left(2 + 3 e^{3}\\right)}{1 + e^{3}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTreat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x^{2} + y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} y\\right)&=x^{2}\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=x^{2} + \\frac{e^{x^{2} + y}}{e^{x^{2} + y} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(1\\right)^{2} + \\left(2\\right)=3.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(1,2)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e^{3}}{1 + e^{3}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1 + 2 e^{3}}{1 + e^{3}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703779 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2 x y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(e^{x - 2 y^{2}} + 1 \\right)},\n$$\n\nevaluate both partial derivatives at $(0,1)$.\n\n$f_x(0,1)=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$f_y(0,1)=$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x - 2 y^{2}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x y^{2}\\right)&=2 y^{2}\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(2 y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=2 y^{2} + \\frac{e^{x - 2 y^{2}}}{e^{x - 2 y^{2}} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(0\\right) - 2 \\left(1\\right)^{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(0,1)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{\\left(e^{-2} + 1\\right) e^{2}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3 + 2 e^{2}}{1 + e^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTreat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x - 2 y^{2}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=- 4 y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x y^{2}\\right)&=4 x y\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 4 y\\right)+\\left(4 x y\\right)\\\\\n&=4 x y - \\frac{4 y e^{x - 2 y^{2}}}{e^{x - 2 y^{2}} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(0\\right) - 2 \\left(1\\right)^{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(0,1)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{\\left(e^{-2} + 1\\right) e^{2}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{4}{1 + e^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703792 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2 x^{2} y + \\ln{\\left(e^{x^{2} + y} + 1 \\right)},\n$$\n\nevaluate both partial derivatives at $(1,0)$.\n\n$f_x(1,0)=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$f_y(1,0)=$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x^{2} + y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x^{2} y\\right)&=4 x y\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 x\\right)+\\left(4 x y\\right)\\\\\n&=4 x y + \\frac{2 x e^{x^{2} + y}}{e^{x^{2} + y} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(1\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(1,0)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e}{1 + e}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 e}{1 + e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTreat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x^{2} + y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x^{2} y\\right)&=2 x^{2}\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(2 x^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=2 x^{2} + \\frac{e^{x^{2} + y}}{e^{x^{2} + y} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(1\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(1,0)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e}{1 + e}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 + 3 e}{1 + e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703809 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(1 + e^{- x y} \\right)},\n$$\n\nevaluate both partial derivatives at $(1,-1)$.\n\n$f_x(1,-1)=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$f_y(1,-1)=$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=- x y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=- y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(x y^{2}\\right)&=y^{2}\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- y\\right)+\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=y^{2} - \\frac{y e^{- x y}}{1 + e^{- x y}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=-1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=- \\left(1\\right) \\left(-1\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(1,-1)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e}{1 + e}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1 + 2 e}{1 + e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTreat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=- x y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=- x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x y^{2}\\right)&=2 x y\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- x\\right)+\\left(2 x y\\right)\\\\\n&=2 x y - \\frac{x e^{- x y}}{1 + e^{- x y}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=-1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=- \\left(1\\right) \\left(-1\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(1,-1)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e}{1 + e}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{- 3 e - 2}{1 + e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703810 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^{2} y + \\ln{\\left(e^{2 x^{2} + y} + 1 \\right)},\n$$\n\nevaluate both partial derivatives at $(2,1)$.\n\n$f_x(2,1)=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$f_y(2,1)=$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=2 x^{2} + y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=4 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(x^{2} y\\right)&=2 x y\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(4 x\\right)+\\left(2 x y\\right)\\\\\n&=2 x y + \\frac{4 x e^{2 x^{2} + y}}{e^{2 x^{2} + y} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=2 \\left(2\\right)^{2} + \\left(1\\right)=9.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(2,1)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(8\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e^{9}}{1 + e^{9}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(8\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{4 \\left(1 + 3 e^{9}\\right)}{1 + e^{9}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTreat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=2 x^{2} + y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} y\\right)&=x^{2}\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=x^{2} + \\frac{e^{2 x^{2} + y}}{e^{2 x^{2} + y} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=2 \\left(2\\right)^{2} + \\left(1\\right)=9.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(2,1)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e^{9}}{1 + e^{9}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{4 + 5 e^{9}}{1 + e^{9}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703816 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sin t,\\qquad y=\\cot t,\\qquad 0<t<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx=\\sin t & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\sin t=x \\\\\ny=\\cot t & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\cot t=y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t & =1 \\\\\n\\cos^2 t & =1-\\sin^2 t \\\\\n\\cos t & =\\sqrt{1-\\sin^2 t}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\cos t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a cotangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\cot t=\\frac{\\cos t}{\\sin t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-\\sin^2 t}}{\\sin t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin t=x$ and $\\cot t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\sqrt{- x^{2} + 1}}{x}.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{- x^{2} + 1}}{x}.$\n\nThe traced curve also satisfies $0<x<1\\quad y>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703823 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos t,\\qquad y=2\\tan t,\\qquad 0<t<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx=2\\cos t & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\cos t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\\ny=2\\tan t & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\tan t=\\frac{y}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t & =1 \\\\\n\\sin^2 t & =1-\\cos^2 t \\\\\n\\sin t & =\\sqrt{1-\\cos^2 t}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\sin t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\tan t=\\frac{\\sin t}{\\cos t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-\\cos^2 t}}{\\cos t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\tan t=\\frac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{4-x^2}}{2}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive. Substituting this root and cancelling the common scale gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2}}{\\frac{x}{2}} \\\\\ny & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{- x^{2} + 4}}{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{- x^{2} + 4}}{x}.$\n\nThe traced curve also satisfies $0<x<2\\quad y>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703827 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\sin t,\\qquad y=2\\tan t,\\qquad 0<t<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx=2\\sin t & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\sin t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\\ny=2\\tan t & \\Rightarrow\\quad \\tan t=\\frac{y}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t & =1 \\\\\n\\cos^2 t & =1-\\sin^2 t \\\\\n\\cos t & =\\sqrt{1-\\sin^2 t}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\cos t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\tan t=\\frac{\\sin t}{\\cos t}=\\frac{\\sin t}{\\sqrt{1-\\sin^2 t}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\tan t=\\frac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{4-x^2}}{2}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive. Substituting this root and cancelling the common scale gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{\\frac{x}{2}}{\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2}} \\\\\ny & =\\frac{2 x}{\\sqrt{- x^{2} + 4}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{2 x}{\\sqrt{- x^{2} + 4}}.$\n\nThe traced curve also satisfies $0<x<2\\quad y>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703942 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{t - 4},\\quad y=- \\frac{t}{2 t + 1},\\quad t\\ne - \\frac{1}{2},\\quad t\\ne 4.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation is\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}$,\\quad x\\ne 0,\\quad x\\ne$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Eliminate the parameter by using the $x$ equation to isolate $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{t - 4} \\\\ t &=4 + \\frac{2}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $t=4 + \\frac{2}{x}$ into the original $y$ expression, then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=-\\left(\\frac{(4 + \\frac{2}{x})}{2 (4 + \\frac{2}{x}) + 1}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{4 + \\frac{2}{x}}{9 + \\frac{4}{x}} \\\\ &=\\frac{(-4 - \\frac{2}{x})(x)}{(9 + \\frac{4}{x})(x)} \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 (2 x + 1)}{9 x + 4} \\\\ &=\\frac{- 4 x - 2}{9 x + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reciprocal $x$ expression never equals $0$. The other excluded parameter value $t=- \\frac{1}{2}$ would give $x=- \\frac{4}{9}$, so it must also be excluded. The simplified formula alone does not retain every missing point.\n\nTherefore the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{- 4 x - 2}{9 x + 4},\\quad x\\ne - \\frac{4}{9},\\quad x\\ne 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703945 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=- \\frac{2}{t + 3},\\quad y=- \\frac{t}{1 - 2 t},\\quad t\\ne -3,\\quad t\\ne \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation is\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}$,\\quad x\\ne 0,\\quad x\\ne$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Eliminate the parameter by using the $x$ equation to isolate $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x &=- \\frac{2}{t + 3} \\\\ t &=-3 - \\frac{2}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $t=-3 - \\frac{2}{x}$ into the original $y$ expression, then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=-\\left(\\frac{(-3 - \\frac{2}{x})}{1 - 2 (-3 - \\frac{2}{x})}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{-3 - \\frac{2}{x}}{7 + \\frac{4}{x}} \\\\ &=\\frac{(3 + \\frac{2}{x})(x)}{(7 + \\frac{4}{x})(x)} \\\\ &=- \\frac{- 3 x - 2}{7 x + 4} \\\\ &=\\frac{3 x + 2}{7 x + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reciprocal $x$ expression never equals $0$. The other excluded parameter value $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ would give $x=- \\frac{4}{7}$, so it must also be excluded. The simplified formula alone does not retain every missing point.\n\nTherefore the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3 x + 2}{7 x + 4},\\quad x\\ne - \\frac{4}{7},\\quad x\\ne 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703952 :question/problem "Given the vector field\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(x,y,z)&=-\\bigl(x y^{2} + 2 y z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad-\\bigl(x^{2} y - x z^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j \\\\ &\\quad-\\bigl(2 x y + 2 x z + y^{2} z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ at $(1,1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j+R\\,\\mathbf k$. The middle cofactor is negative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ \\partial/\\partial x&\\partial/\\partial y&\\partial/\\partial z \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTake the cross partial derivatives, holding the other coordinates constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=\\bigl(- 2 x - 2 y z\\bigr)-\\bigl(2 x z\\bigr) \\\\ &=- 2 x z - 2 x - 2 y z \\\\ P_z-R_x&=\\bigl(- 2 y\\bigr)-\\bigl(- 2 y - 2 z\\bigr) \\\\ &=2 z \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=\\bigl(- 2 x y + z^{2}\\bigr)-\\bigl(- 2 x y - 2 z\\bigr) \\\\ &=z^{2} + 2 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F&=-\\bigl(2 x z + 2 x + 2 y z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+2 z\\,\\mathbf j \\\\ &\\quad+\\bigl(z^{2} + 2 z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the point into these derivatives, not into the original field:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=- 2 (1) (-1) - 2 (1) - 2 (1) (-1) \\\\ &=2 \\\\ P_z-R_x&=2 (-1) \\\\ &=-2 \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=(-1)^{2} + 2 (-1) \\\\ &=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)(1,1,-1)=2\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-\\,\\mathbf k\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703956 :question/problem "Given the vector field\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(x,y,z)&=-\\bigl(x z^{2} + 2 y^{2} z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+\\bigl(2 x^{2} y + 2 x z - y z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j \\\\ &\\quad+\\bigl(x^{2} z - 2 x y\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ at $(0,-2,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j+R\\,\\mathbf k$. The middle cofactor is negative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ \\partial/\\partial x&\\partial/\\partial y&\\partial/\\partial z \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTake the cross partial derivatives, holding the other coordinates constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=\\bigl(- 2 x\\bigr)-\\bigl(2 x - y\\bigr) \\\\ &=- 4 x + y \\\\ P_z-R_x&=\\bigl(- 2 x z - 2 y^{2}\\bigr)-\\bigl(2 x z - 2 y\\bigr) \\\\ &=- 4 x z - 2 y^{2} + 2 y \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=\\bigl(4 x y + 2 z\\bigr)-\\bigl(- 4 y z\\bigr) \\\\ &=4 x y + 4 y z + 2 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F&=-\\bigl(4 x - y\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad-\\bigl(4 x z + 2 y^{2} - 2 y\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j \\\\ &\\quad+\\bigl(4 x y + 4 y z + 2 z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the point into these derivatives, not into the original field:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=- 4 (0) + (-2) \\\\ &=-2 \\\\ P_z-R_x&=- 4 (0) (1) - 2 (-2)^{2} + 2 (-2) \\\\ &=-12 \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=4 (0) (-2) + 4 (-2) (1) + 2 (1) \\\\ &=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)(0,-2,1)=-2\\,\\mathbf i-12\\,\\mathbf j-6\\,\\mathbf k\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703962 :question/problem "Given the vector field\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(x,y,z)&=-\\bigl(x y^{2} + 2 y z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad-\\bigl(x^{2} y + 2 x z^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j \\\\ &\\quad+\\bigl(2 x y - 2 x z + 2 y^{2} z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ at $(-2,-2,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j+R\\,\\mathbf k$. The middle cofactor is negative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ \\partial/\\partial x&\\partial/\\partial y&\\partial/\\partial z \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTake the cross partial derivatives, holding the other coordinates constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=\\bigl(2 x + 4 y z\\bigr)-\\bigl(- 4 x z\\bigr) \\\\ &=4 x z + 2 x + 4 y z \\\\ P_z-R_x&=\\bigl(- 2 y\\bigr)-\\bigl(2 y - 2 z\\bigr) \\\\ &=- 4 y + 2 z \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=\\bigl(- 2 x y - 2 z^{2}\\bigr)-\\bigl(- 2 x y - 2 z\\bigr) \\\\ &=- 2 z^{2} + 2 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(4 x z + 2 x + 4 y z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad-\\bigl(4 y - 2 z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j \\\\ &\\quad-\\bigl(2 z^{2} - 2 z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the point into these derivatives, not into the original field:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=4 (-2) (2) + 2 (-2) + 4 (-2) (2) \\\\ &=-36 \\\\ P_z-R_x&=- 4 (-2) + 2 (2) \\\\ &=12 \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=- 2 (2)^{2} + 2 (2) \\\\ &=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)(-2,-2,2)=-36\\,\\mathbf i+12\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703967 :question/problem "Given the vector field\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(x,y,z)&=-\\bigl(2 x y + 2 x z^{2} + 2 y z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+\\bigl(2 x z + 2 y z^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j \\\\ &\\quad+\\bigl(x^{2} y + 2 y^{2} z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ at $(0,2,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j+R\\,\\mathbf k$. The middle cofactor is negative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ \\partial/\\partial x&\\partial/\\partial y&\\partial/\\partial z \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTake the cross partial derivatives, holding the other coordinates constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=\\bigl(x^{2} + 4 y z\\bigr)-\\bigl(2 x + 4 y z\\bigr) \\\\ &=x^{2} - 2 x \\\\ P_z-R_x&=\\bigl(- 4 x z - 2 y\\bigr)-\\bigl(2 x y\\bigr) \\\\ &=- 2 x y - 4 x z - 2 y \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=\\bigl(2 z\\bigr)-\\bigl(- 2 x - 2 z\\bigr) \\\\ &=2 x + 4 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(x^{2} - 2 x\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad-\\bigl(2 x y + 4 x z + 2 y\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j \\\\ &\\quad+\\bigl(2 x + 4 z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the point into these derivatives, not into the original field:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=(0)^{2} - 2 (0) \\\\ &=0 \\\\ P_z-R_x&=- 2 (0) (2) - 4 (0) (-1) - 2 (2) \\\\ &=-4 \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=2 (0) + 4 (-1) \\\\ &=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)(0,2,-1)=-4\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704508 :question/problem "Given the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(x^{2} + 2 y \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(2 x e^{x y} + y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$, defined where $x+y>0$, evaluate its scalar curl at $(2,1)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, the **scalar curl** is $Q_x-P_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. Both cross-partials require the product rule and the chain rule; retain both terms from each product.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_x&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x e^{x y} + y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=2 x y e^{x y} + 2 e^{x y}\\\\\nP_y&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 y}{x + y} + 2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(2 x y e^{x y} + 2 e^{x y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{2 y}{x + y} + 2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=2 x y e^{x y} - \\frac{2 y}{x + y} + 2 e^{x y} - 2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate first, then substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(Q_x-P_y)(2,1)&=\\left(6 e^{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{2}{3} + 2 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} - \\frac{2}{3} + 6 e^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704512 :question/problem "Given the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- 2 y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(2 x \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$, defined where $x+y>0$, evaluate its scalar curl at $(-1,3)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, the **scalar curl** is $Q_x-P_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. Both cross-partials require the product rule and the chain rule; retain both terms from each product.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_x&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 x}{x + y} + 2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\\\\nP_y&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- 2 y e^{x y}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 x y e^{x y} - 2 e^{x y}\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(\\frac{2 x}{x + y} + 2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)-\\left(- 2 x y e^{x y} - 2 e^{x y}\\right)\\\\\n&=2 x y e^{x y} + \\frac{2 x}{x + y} + 2 e^{x y} + 2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate first, then substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(Q_x-P_y)(-1,3)&=\\left(-1 + 2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{4}{e^{3}}\\right)\\\\\n&=-1 - \\frac{4}{e^{3}} + 2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704524 :question/problem "Given the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- 3 y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(2 x \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$, defined where $x+y>0$, evaluate its scalar curl at $(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, the **scalar curl** is $Q_x-P_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. Both cross-partials require the product rule and the chain rule; retain both terms from each product.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_x&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 x}{x + y} + 2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\\\\nP_y&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- 3 y e^{x y}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 3 x y e^{x y} - 3 e^{x y}\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(\\frac{2 x}{x + y} + 2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)-\\left(- 3 x y e^{x y} - 3 e^{x y}\\right)\\\\\n&=3 x y e^{x y} + \\frac{2 x}{x + y} + 3 e^{x y} + 2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate first, then substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(Q_x-P_y)(1,1)&=\\left(1 + 2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\right)-\\left(- 6 e\\right)\\\\\n&=1 + 2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 6 e\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704525 :question/problem "Given the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(x^{2} + 3 y \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- 2 x e^{x y} + y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$, defined where $x+y>0$, evaluate its scalar curl at $(1,2)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, the **scalar curl** is $Q_x-P_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. Both cross-partials require the product rule and the chain rule; retain both terms from each product.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_x&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- 2 x e^{x y} + y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 x y e^{x y} - 2 e^{x y}\\\\\nP_y&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} + 3 y \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3 y}{x + y} + 3 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- 2 x y e^{x y} - 2 e^{x y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 y}{x + y} + 3 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 x y e^{x y} - \\frac{3 y}{x + y} - 2 e^{x y} - 3 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate first, then substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(Q_x-P_y)(1,2)&=\\left(- 6 e^{2}\\right)-\\left(2 + 3 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 6 e^{2} - 3 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704559 :question/problem "![Vector field; arrows share one scale.](images/fb/fbc0162b00e13fece37b6fb1fbd932f2110037f40f84280b8b4a4b1519107c0c.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page. Both planar components are affine functions, and the displayed pattern continues throughout the domain.\n\nAll vectors are horizontal. The horizontal component increases at a constant rate as $y$ increases, and does not change with the other coordinate.\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Imagine a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe horizontal flow is more rightward above a wheel than below it. This contributes a **clockwise** turn.\n\nThe vertical flow is unchanged between the right and left sides of a wheel, so it contributes no rotation.\n\nThe wheel turns **clockwise** everywhere, so $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere. Wrap the fingers of your right hand clockwise: the thumb points into the page, in the **negative $z$-direction**. The field is **not irrotational**.\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational: **false**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **true**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **false**.\n\nThe comparison below contrasts unequal sideways pushes with equal pushes.\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704560 :question/problem "![Vector field; arrows share one scale.](images/62/6265f01b567c086293268e04747e11146dbfa37ad5a1dfbe24f3199eb57ddeff.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page. Both planar components are affine functions, and the displayed pattern continues throughout the domain.\n\nAll vectors are vertical. The vertical component increases at a constant rate as $y$ increases, and does not change with the other coordinate.\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Imagine a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe horizontal flow is unchanged between the upper and lower sides of a wheel, so it contributes no rotation.\n\nThe vertical flow is unchanged between the right and left sides of a wheel, so it contributes no rotation.\n\nChanges of horizontal flow along the horizontal axis and vertical flow along the vertical axis stretch or compress the flow, but add no paddlewheel rotation.\n\nThe wheel does not spin. Thus $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere, and $\\mathbf F$ is **irrotational**. The zero vector has no direction.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$ points in the negative $z$-direction everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational: **true**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **true**.\n\nThe comparison below contrasts unequal sideways pushes with equal pushes.\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704575 :question/problem "![Vector field; arrows share one scale.](images/68/688d9a7587ff23cad27de658b4d4e05b7600cf25126ef2b17b30f32fba11df24.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page. Both planar components are affine functions, and the displayed pattern continues throughout the domain.\n\nAll vectors are vertical. The vertical component increases at a constant rate as $x$ increases, and does not change with the other coordinate.\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Imagine a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe horizontal flow is unchanged between the upper and lower sides of a wheel, so it contributes no rotation.\n\nThe vertical flow is more upward to the right of a wheel than to its left. This contributes a **counterclockwise** turn.\n\nThe wheel turns **counterclockwise** everywhere, so $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere. Wrap the fingers of your right hand counterclockwise: the thumb points out of the page, in the **positive $z$-direction**. The field is **not irrotational**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational: **false**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **true**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction everywhere: **true**.\n\nThe comparison below contrasts unequal sideways pushes with equal pushes.\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704585 :question/problem "![Vector field; arrows share one scale.](images/01/013ee8bc237f52d5487745d32840917046ad59f7c6b1848327e65ad463c7a4ff.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page. Both planar components are affine functions, and the displayed pattern continues throughout the domain.\n\nAll vectors are horizontal. The horizontal component decreases at a constant rate as $y$ increases, and does not change with the other coordinate.\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Imagine a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe horizontal flow is more leftward above a wheel than below it. This contributes a **counterclockwise** turn.\n\nThe vertical flow is unchanged between the right and left sides of a wheel, so it contributes no rotation.\n\nA constant flow can carry the wheel along without changing its spin.\n\nThe wheel turns **counterclockwise** everywhere, so $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere. Wrap the fingers of your right hand counterclockwise: the thumb points out of the page, in the **positive $z$-direction**. The field is **not irrotational**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **true**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational: **false**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$ points in the negative $z$-direction everywhere: **false**.\n\nThe comparison below contrasts unequal sideways pushes with equal pushes.\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704762 :question/problem "The curve $C$ follows the straight sides of a square in the order\n\n$$\n(1,0)\\longrightarrow(1,1)\\longrightarrow(0,1)\\longrightarrow(0,0)\\longrightarrow(1,0).\n$$\n\nLet $D$ be its interior.\n\nUse Green's theorem to choose the function $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\oint_C\\left(x^{2} y - 2 y \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&\\quad+\\left(- 2 x y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The traversal keeps the interior on the left, so it is **counterclockwise**. Green's theorem applies because both coefficients have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$. For this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n![The region and oriented boundary.](images/aa/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nIdentify the coefficients of the two differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=x^{2} y - 2 y \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\\\Q(x,y)&=- 2 x y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHold the other variable constant when taking each partial derivative. Apply the product and chain rules where required.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=x^{2} - 2 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\\\Q_x&=- 2 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative in the order required by the traversal, then collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=Q_x-P_y\\\\&=\\left(- 2 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(x^{2} - 2 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=- x^{2} + 4 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + 4 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$. No evaluation of the double integral is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704763 :question/problem "The curve $C$ follows the straight sides of a square in the order\n\n$$\n(0,0)\\longrightarrow(1,0)\\longrightarrow(1,1)\\longrightarrow(0,1)\\longrightarrow(0,0).\n$$\n\nLet $D$ be its interior.\n\nUse Green's theorem to choose the function $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\oint_C\\left(y^{2} + y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&\\quad+\\left(x e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The traversal keeps the interior on the left, so it is **counterclockwise**. Green's theorem applies because both coefficients have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$. For this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n![The region and oriented boundary.](images/aa/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nIdentify the coefficients of the two differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=y^{2} + y e^{x y},\\\\Q(x,y)&=x e^{x y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHold the other variable constant when taking each partial derivative. Apply the product and chain rules where required.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=x y e^{x y} + 2 y + e^{x y},\\\\Q_x&=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative in the order required by the traversal, then collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=Q_x-P_y\\\\&=\\left(x y e^{x y} + e^{x y}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(x y e^{x y} + 2 y + e^{x y}\\right)\\\\&=- 2 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(x,y)=- 2 y$. No evaluation of the double integral is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704768 :question/problem "The curve $C$ follows the straight sides of a square in the order\n\n$$\n(0,1)\\longrightarrow(1,1)\\longrightarrow(1,0)\\longrightarrow(0,0)\\longrightarrow(0,1).\n$$\n\nLet $D$ be its interior.\n\nUse Green's theorem to choose the function $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\oint_C\\left(- x^{2} y + 2 y \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&\\quad+\\left(2 x y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The traversal keeps the interior on the right, so it is **clockwise**. Green's theorem applies because both coefficients have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$. Reversing the positively oriented boundary changes the sign, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=-\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(P_y-Q_x)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![The region and oriented boundary.](images/6e/6e8981112aadeb2bd6d8c9ba2d369ccad4fda1ca2ac12dc7789022b2530d4891.png)\n\nIdentify the coefficients of the two differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=- x^{2} y + 2 y \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\\\Q(x,y)&=2 x y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHold the other variable constant when taking each partial derivative. Apply the product and chain rules where required.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=- x^{2} + 2 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\\\Q_x&=2 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative in the order required by the traversal, then collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=P_y-Q_x\\\\&=\\left(- x^{2} + 2 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(2 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=- x^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(x,y)=- x^{2}$. No evaluation of the double integral is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704771 :question/problem "The curve $C$ follows the straight sides of a square in the order\n\n$$\n(1,0)\\longrightarrow(0,0)\\longrightarrow(0,1)\\longrightarrow(1,1)\\longrightarrow(1,0).\n$$\n\nLet $D$ be its interior.\n\nUse Green's theorem to choose the function $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\oint_C\\left(2 y^{2} e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&\\quad+\\left(- x^{2} y + 2 x y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The traversal keeps the interior on the right, so it is **clockwise**. Green's theorem applies because both coefficients have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$. Reversing the positively oriented boundary changes the sign, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=-\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(P_y-Q_x)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![The region and oriented boundary.](images/6e/6e8981112aadeb2bd6d8c9ba2d369ccad4fda1ca2ac12dc7789022b2530d4891.png)\n\nIdentify the coefficients of the two differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=2 y^{2} e^{x y},\\\\Q(x,y)&=- x^{2} y + 2 x y e^{x y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHold the other variable constant when taking each partial derivative. Apply the product and chain rules where required.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=2 x y^{2} e^{x y} + 4 y e^{x y},\\\\Q_x&=2 x y^{2} e^{x y} - 2 x y + 2 y e^{x y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative in the order required by the traversal, then collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=P_y-Q_x\\\\&=\\left(2 x y^{2} e^{x y} + 4 y e^{x y}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(2 x y^{2} e^{x y} - 2 x y + 2 y e^{x y}\\right)\\\\&=2 x y + 2 y e^{x y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(x,y)=2 x y + 2 y e^{x y}$. No evaluation of the double integral is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704801 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the unit square, traversed counterclockwise, and let $D$ be its interior.\n\nUse Green's theorem to choose the function $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\oint_C\\left(y^{2} + 2 y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&\\quad+\\left(- x^{2} y + 2 x e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The stated traversal is **counterclockwise**. Green's theorem applies because both coefficients have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$. For this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n![The region and oriented boundary.](images/aa/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nIdentify the coefficients of the two differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=y^{2} + 2 y e^{x y},\\\\Q(x,y)&=- x^{2} y + 2 x e^{x y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHold the other variable constant when taking each partial derivative. Apply the product and chain rules where required.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=2 x y e^{x y} + 2 y + 2 e^{x y},\\\\Q_x&=2 x y e^{x y} - 2 x y + 2 e^{x y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative in the order required by the traversal, then collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=Q_x-P_y\\\\&=\\left(2 x y e^{x y} - 2 x y + 2 e^{x y}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(2 x y e^{x y} + 2 y + 2 e^{x y}\\right)\\\\&=- 2 x y - 2 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(x,y)=- 2 x y - 2 y$. No evaluation of the double integral is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704806 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the unit square, traversed counterclockwise, and let $D$ be its interior.\n\nUse Green's theorem to choose the function $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\oint_C\\left(x^{2} y + 2 y \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&\\quad+\\left(- 2 x y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The stated traversal is **counterclockwise**. Green's theorem applies because both coefficients have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$. For this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n![The region and oriented boundary.](images/aa/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nIdentify the coefficients of the two differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=x^{2} y + 2 y \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\\\Q(x,y)&=- 2 x y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHold the other variable constant when taking each partial derivative. Apply the product and chain rules where required.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=x^{2} + 2 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\\\Q_x&=- 2 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative in the order required by the traversal, then collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=Q_x-P_y\\\\&=\\left(- 2 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(x^{2} + 2 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=- x^{2} - 4 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 4 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(x,y)=- x^{2} - 4 y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 4 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$. No evaluation of the double integral is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704811 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the unit square, traversed counterclockwise, and let $D$ be its interior.\n\nUse Green's theorem to choose the function $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\oint_C\\left(y^{2} e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&\\quad+\\left(x^{2} y - x y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The stated traversal is **counterclockwise**. Green's theorem applies because both coefficients have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$. For this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n![The region and oriented boundary.](images/aa/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nIdentify the coefficients of the two differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=y^{2} e^{x y},\\\\Q(x,y)&=x^{2} y - x y e^{x y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHold the other variable constant when taking each partial derivative. Apply the product and chain rules where required.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=x y^{2} e^{x y} + 2 y e^{x y},\\\\Q_x&=- x y^{2} e^{x y} + 2 x y - y e^{x y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative in the order required by the traversal, then collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=Q_x-P_y\\\\&=\\left(- x y^{2} e^{x y} + 2 x y - y e^{x y}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(x y^{2} e^{x y} + 2 y e^{x y}\\right)\\\\&=- 2 x y^{2} e^{x y} + 2 x y - 3 y e^{x y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(x,y)=- 2 x y^{2} e^{x y} + 2 x y - 3 y e^{x y}$. No evaluation of the double integral is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704815 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the unit square, traversed counterclockwise, and let $D$ be its interior.\n\nUse Green's theorem to choose the function $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\oint_C\\left(- y^{2} + y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&\\quad+\\left(x^{2} y + x e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The stated traversal is **counterclockwise**. Green's theorem applies because both coefficients have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$. For this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n![The region and oriented boundary.](images/aa/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nIdentify the coefficients of the two differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=- y^{2} + y e^{x y},\\\\Q(x,y)&=x^{2} y + x e^{x y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHold the other variable constant when taking each partial derivative. Apply the product and chain rules where required.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=x y e^{x y} - 2 y + e^{x y},\\\\Q_x&=x y e^{x y} + 2 x y + e^{x y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative in the order required by the traversal, then collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=Q_x-P_y\\\\&=\\left(x y e^{x y} + 2 x y + e^{x y}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(x y e^{x y} - 2 y + e^{x y}\\right)\\\\&=2 x y + 2 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(x,y)=2 x y + 2 y$. No evaluation of the double integral is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704829 :question/problem "Calculate \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{8}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n{{antiderivative}}" :question/worked-solution "Apply the power rule with $n=8$ and include an arbitrary constant $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x^{8}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\frac{x^{8+1}}{8+1}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{9}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{9}}{9}+C\\right)&=\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 9x^{8}+0 \\\\ &=x^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704833 :question/problem "Calculate \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{15}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n{{antiderivative}}" :question/worked-solution "Apply the power rule with $n=15$ and include an arbitrary constant $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x^{15}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\frac{x^{15+1}}{15+1}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{16}}{16}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{16}}{16}+C\\right)&=\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot 16x^{15}+0 \\\\ &=x^{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704835 :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{19}$ and ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x).$ Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n$F(x)=$ {{antiderivative}}" :question/worked-solution "Integration reverses differentiation, so $F'(x)=f(x)$ gives $F(x)=\\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nApply the power rule with $n=19$ and include an arbitrary constant $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x)&=\\int x^{19}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{19+1}}{19+1}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{20}}{20}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{20}}{20}+C\\right)&=\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot 20x^{19}+0 \\\\ &=x^{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704837 :question/problem "Calculate \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n{{antiderivative}}" :question/worked-solution "Apply the power rule with $n=1$ and include an arbitrary constant $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\frac{x^{1+1}}{1+1}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}+C\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\ &=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704878 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\left(- x^{2} + 2 x y + 1\\right)\\,dx+\\left(- x^{2} - y^{2} + 1\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices $\\left( 0, \\  0\\right)$, $\\left( 3, \\  3\\right)$, $\\left( 0, \\  3\\right)$, traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve. The components have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, so\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=- x^{2} + 2 x y + 1$ and $Q=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=2 x,\\\\\nQ_x&=- 2 x,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=- 4 x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sloping side is $y=x$. Vertical slices start there and end at $y=3$.\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\ x\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nThe shaded region and counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/56/5646c2962c3d7aa65c0dd251bdfd83ffb804673574b0398c4bddb4bab0c26c82.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy&=\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{x}^{3}\\left(- 4 x\\right)\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{3}\\left[- 4 x y\\right]_{x}^{3}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(4 x^{2} - 12 x\\right)\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} - 6 x^{2}\\right]_{0}^{3}\\\\\n&=-18-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=-18.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704886 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\left(2 x^{2} + 3 x y + 1\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 1\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices $\\left( 0, \\  0\\right)$, $\\left( 1, \\  -2\\right)$, $\\left( 1, \\  2\\right)$, traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve. The components have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, so\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=2 x^{2} + 3 x y + 1$ and $Q=3 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=3 x,\\\\\nQ_x&=6 x,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=3 x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two sloping sides are $y=- 2 x$ and $y=2 x$. Use vertical slices.\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\ - 2 x\\le y\\le 2 x\\}.\n$$\n\nThe shaded region and counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/82/822d9e5847362edbf83c49451885ab5af5a3a286ad82ac5f992ddc0a406a6e64.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{- 2 x}^{2 x}\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[3 x y\\right]_{- 2 x}^{2 x}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(12 x^{2}\\right)\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[4 x^{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=4-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704895 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\left(- x^{2} + 2 x y + 1\\right)\\,dx+\\left(- 3 x^{2} - y^{2} + 1\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices $\\left( 0, \\  0\\right)$, $\\left( 1, \\  -3\\right)$, $\\left( 1, \\  3\\right)$, traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve. The components have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, so\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=- x^{2} + 2 x y + 1$ and $Q=- 3 x^{2} - y^{2} + 1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=2 x,\\\\\nQ_x&=- 6 x,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=- 8 x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two sloping sides are $y=- 3 x$ and $y=3 x$. Use vertical slices.\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\ - 3 x\\le y\\le 3 x\\}.\n$$\n\nThe shaded region and counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/1a/1a917e1bc779206e3db6cb92f329cc4880b0eded367a1220a34a4668cd3301d8.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{- 3 x}^{3 x}\\left(- 8 x\\right)\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[- 8 x y\\right]_{- 3 x}^{3 x}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 48 x^{2}\\right)\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[- 16 x^{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=-16-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=-16.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704897 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\left(2 x^{2} + 3 x y + 1\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 1\\right)\\,dy\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices $\\left( 0, \\  0\\right)$, $\\left( 3, \\  3\\right)$, $\\left( 0, \\  3\\right)$, traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve. The components have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, so\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=2 x^{2} + 3 x y + 1$ and $Q=2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=3 x,\\\\\nQ_x&=4 x,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sloping side is $y=x$. Vertical slices start there and end at $y=3$.\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\ x\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nThe shaded region and counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/56/5646c2962c3d7aa65c0dd251bdfd83ffb804673574b0398c4bddb4bab0c26c82.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy&=\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{x}^{3}\\left(x\\right)\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{3}\\left[x y\\right]_{x}^{3}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(- x^{2} + 3 x\\right)\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + \\frac{3 x^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{3}\\\\\n&=\\frac{9}{2}-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{9}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704968 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the rectangle $-1\\le y\\le 0,\\quad 1\\le x\\le 3$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nUse Green's theorem:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $x$ first, represents the following line integral? Use the actual region $D$ with bounds in increasing order. Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The component functions are continuously differentiable on an open set containing $D$. For counterclockwise traversal, Green's theorem uses $Q_x-P_y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=y^{2},\\\\Q&=- x y,\\\\P_y&=2 y,\\\\Q_x&=- y,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(- y)-(2 y)\\\\&=- 3 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $D$ is described by $-1\\le y\\le 0,\\quad 1\\le x\\le 3$. The bounds stay attached to their variables.\n\n![](images/ed/ed20fa7bcafeb09c64cf3b606fd664f552183dd2743f6c4e803ffee48a7dfde2.png)\n\nThe required setup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\int_{-1}^{0}\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
